\details{
\strong{What Parquet cannot hold.} Fitted models (BART, INLA), nested lists, and
other non-rectangular objects have no Parquet representation. Asking for a
tabular format on one of those records an error for that file and moves on —
the convention is to keep those as \code{qs2}/\code{rds}, so \code{to = "rds"} converts them
without leaving the family. A mixed directory therefore converts in one call:
the tables land as Parquet, the model fits report why they did not.

\strong{Spatial objects.} An \code{sf} object written to \code{"parquet"} becomes GeoParquet
via \code{sfarrow}, keeping its geometry and CRS. \code{"geojson"}, \code{"gpkg"}, and
\code{"shp"} are spatial-only. Formats that cannot carry geometry (\code{"csv"},
\code{"fst"}, \code{"xlsx"}, …) write the attribute table and warn that they dropped it.

\strong{Reading.} \code{.qs2} is read with \code{qs2::qs_read()} and legacy \code{.qs} with
\code{qs::qread()}; \code{qs2} detects a legacy file and refuses it, so the reader falls
back to the other package when an extension turns out to be misleading.
Reading a legacy \code{.qs} therefore needs the \code{qs} package installed, which is a
separate package from \code{qs2}.

\strong{And \code{qs} is getting hard to install, which is the whole argument for
converting.} As of September 2026 \code{qs} is archived on CRAN, and its last
version (0.27.3, which is also the GitHub head) does not compile against
R 4.6: it calls \code{SET_FRAME()}, which R 4.5 still declared as "used by mmap
qs" and R 4.6 dropped from the API. A binary built under R 4.5 crashes the
session if loaded under R 4.6. So a \code{.qs} file is readable only on an R old
enough to still build its reader, and that window is closing. Convert on the
oldest R that can still open the file, not later.

\strong{Failures do not stop the batch.} A file that cannot be read or written
raises a warning and lands in the returned frame with \code{status = "error"} and
the reason, so one unconvertible model fit does not abort a directory of
tables. Check \code{status} rather than assuming every row was written.
